Java Code:
abstract class BankAccount {
    abstract void calculateInterest();
    void openAccount() {
        System.out.println("Account opened successfully.");
    }
}
class SavingsAccount extends BankAccount {
    void calculateInterest() {
        System.out.println("Savings Account: Interest = 4% per annum");
    }
}
class FixedDeposit extends BankAccount {
    void calculateInterest() {
        System.out.println("Fixed Deposit: Interest = 6.5% per annum");
    }
}
public class BankSystem {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        BankAccount acc1 = new SavingsAccount();
        BankAccount acc2 = new FixedDeposit();
        acc1.openAccount();
        acc1.calculateInterest();
        acc2.openAccount();
        acc2.calculateInterest();
    }
}
Explanation:
BankAccount is an abstract class with a common method openAccount() and an abstract method calculateInterest().
Subclasses SavingsAccount and FixedDeposit override the abstract method to provide specific logic.
This demonstrates abstraction, where the interface is fixed (calculateInterest() must be implemented), but the implementation differs.
Benefits of Abstraction:
Promotes flexibility in design
Enhances code extensibility�new account types can be added easily
Enforces a contract: every account must implement calculateInterest()
Reduces complexity by exposing only relevant behavior

